WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.119 --> 00:00:09.659
Welcome to Jev Flow: a standalone, open-source studio for building
typed AI workflows with clear, code-owned outcomes.

2
00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:18.897
A model returns structured evidence; your flow validates it, applies
explicit rules, and chooses the next step.

3
00:00:20.301 --> 00:00:28.401
Clone the repository, install Node dependencies, and start the local
server. No provider key is needed to explore fixtures.

4
00:00:29.897 --> 00:00:37.677
Open the Studio to inspect a flow as connected, editable steps. Each
node has a visible input and output.

5
00:00:39.119 --> 00:00:46.939
Ask bounded Choice, Noul, or Score questions, then validate the answer
against its declared type before routing.

6
00:00:54.185 --> 00:01:01.965
The preview uses labeled synthetic answers, so you can reproduce
branches without calling a model or triggering external actions.

7
00:01:03.395 --> 00:01:12.135
For live runs, connect a supported provider explicitly. The trace
identifies source and model, and reports measured latency.

8
00:01:13.552 --> 00:01:23.052
If credentials are missing or output is invalid, the run stays
unavailable or fails validation. Unknown costs stay unknown.

9
00:01:24.475 --> 00:01:35.375
The Compendium generates 388,080 certified configurations on demand
from reusable patterns, domains, variants, and thresholds.

10
00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:45.939
Search, inspect, and install a configuration as a starting point; this
count is not 388,080 model executions.

11
00:01:47.358 --> 00:01:54.498
The Battle Arena puts Jev and a configured language model side by
side, with separate answers and provenance.

12
00:01:55.918 --> 00:02:04.458
Compare like-for-like results using observed latency and
provider-reported usage; inconclusive data does not produce a winner.

13
00:02:05.875 --> 00:02:13.535
The Self-Driving Cart is a sandbox for testing lane choices, speed
control, hazards, and safety interventions.

14
00:02:14.959 --> 00:02:22.499
Adjust the course and decision interval, then review distance, action
traces, latency, and available cost.

15
00:02:23.917 --> 00:02:29.417
The simulation is an engineering exercise, not proof of real-world
vehicle safety.

16
00:02:31.024 --> 00:02:38.404
Labs include spam screening, evidence review, chess, Blast Garden, and
rescue exercises.

17
00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:45.409
In game modes, the local rules engine validates proposed moves before
they take effect.

18
00:02:46.827 --> 00:02:54.007
Use examples to shape your own inputs, schemas, thresholds, fallbacks,
and human-review branches.

19
00:02:55.412 --> 00:03:03.432
Jev interprets bounded questions. Your code owns validation, policy,
and every consequential action.

20
00:03:04.865 --> 00:03:12.805
Run locally, inspect every source, and build workflows you can test
and explain. This is Jev Flow.
